# Step 5: this file IS the high tier.
#
# The risk tiers and what they cost are in PIPELINE.md, Step 5. In short: the
# person who prompted a change counts as one of its reviewers, so the medium
# tier's one person is already filled by the author and needs no approval here.
# The high tier's two people are the author plus one more, which is exactly one
# GitHub approval. So "high tier" and "listed in this file" are the same thing,
# and there is no second list to drift out of step with this one.
#
# The approval count stays at 0 because a one-person repo cannot raise it:
# GitHub will not let you approve your own pull request. This file carries the
# rule instead.
#
# The cost is measured, not assumed — and measured the right way. What a path
# costs is not how often it changes; it is how often it changes in a commit
# that touches nothing else already owned. Of the repo's 15 commits, 10 touch
# at least one path below, mostly the workflows while this pipeline was being
# built. `src/storage.ts` adds 2 of those 10: its third change was the initial
# commit, which was owned already. So the scope is wide in gross terms and the
# last addition to it was cheap, and those are two different numbers.
#
# Widening it to the whole repo would still make every pull request wait.

# --- What CI runs ---
#
# CI cannot check itself. The attack is one pull request that disables a check
# and adds the code that check would have caught. Owning the workflow files
# alone does not stop it: `ci.yml` only calls `npm run lint|typecheck|test`,
# and what those commands DO lives elsewhere. All four files below are part of
# that answer, so all four are owned.

/.github/workflows/ @dennisjhao

# `"test": "true"` is a passing test suite that runs nothing.
/package.json @dennisjhao

# These can drop the checks without touching a step: an eslint config that
# ignores `src/`, a tsconfig that excludes it.
/eslint.config.js @dennisjhao
/tsconfig.json @dennisjhao

# Which package `eslint` and `vitest` resolve to. REVIEW.md tells reviewers to
# skip this file, which is the right call for reading a diff and the wrong one
# for deciding who signs it off.
/package-lock.json @dennisjhao

# --- The highest-risk code ---
#
# The article's high tier is "the highest-risk code, plus a small set of paths
# that always need two people". In this repo the first half is one file.
#
# `src/storage.ts` is the only file that touches the disk, and `save()` renames
# over an existing file. A bug here does not produce a wrong answer; it loses
# the user's list. CLAUDE.md already calls the file sensitive, and REVIEW.md
# carries invariants for it that no test guards, because they cannot be tested
# from inside the process.

/src/storage.ts @dennisjhao

# Owning the code and not the test that holds it down is the `ci.yml` mistake
# again: a pull request weakens the test and changes the code behind it. This
# costs nothing extra — all 3 commits that touched `src/storage.ts` touched
# this file in the same commit, and no commit has ever touched it alone.

/tests/storage.test.ts @dennisjhao

# --- This file ---
#
# The file that assigns ownership has to be owned, or a pull request deletes it
# first and changes a workflow second. Same shape as the CLAUDE.md problem: what
# governs review must not be editable by what is being reviewed.

/.github/CODEOWNERS @dennisjhao

# --- Deliberately NOT owned: REVIEW.md and CLAUDE.md ---
#
# They are the reviewer's instructions, so by the logic above they belong here.
#
# This used to say they were left out on cost, because CLAUDE.md changed 6
# times and REVIEW.md 4 in 15 commits. That was the wrong number: only one of
# those commits (9ea5104) touched neither file alongside a path that is already
# owned, so owning both would add about 1 pull request in 15, not half. The
# cost reason does not hold, and it is withdrawn.
#
# What holds is the safety reason: neither file governs the run that judges it
# any more. The AI review's action swaps in main's CLAUDE.md before it runs,
# and both review jobs now swap in main's REVIEW.md and then check that the
# swap held. A pull request's copy of either file is a proposal, visible in the
# diff and reviewable, with no say over its own review. CLAUDE.md's Non-goals
# carries the reasoning; PIPELINE.md records how it was fixed.
#
# One caveat on that 1-in-15: it is low because this exercise happened to batch
# doc edits into the pull requests that changed workflows. A doc-only pull
# request is perfectly normal and #20 was one, so the figure will rise. If it
# does, the choice is worth making again — on the safety reason, which is the
# one that stands.
